      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2010 John Wiley & Sons A/S.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "PROBLEM",
            "content": "Asthma influences pregnancy outcome and pregnancy affects asthma severity, but the immunologic mechanisms of these interactions are not fully elucidated."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHOD",
            "content": "The prevalence of lymphocyte subsets was identified by cell surface markers and intracellular FoxP3 staining, in healthy non-pregnant (HNP; N\u2003=\u200315), healthy pregnant (HP; N=33), asthmatic non-pregnant (ANP; N=62) and asthmatic pregnant (AP; N=61) women."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Regulatory T cell (Treg) prevalence was higher in HP than in HNP subjects and showed a positive correlation with fetal birth weight, which was blunted in AP group. Treg prevalence was lower and invariable natural killer T cell prevalence was higher in AP patients (compared to HP). Higher naive and lower effector T cell prevalence was observed in AP than in ANP group."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSION",
            "content": "Pregnancy-induced increase in Treg cell prevalence is absent in asthmatic pregnancy that may interfere with physiological intrauterine growth. However, pregnancy-specific inhibition of asthmatic inflammation can be detected in uncomplicated asthmatic pregnancy."
          }
        ]
      },
